{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And I have taken the Levites in place of every firstborn.</b> For if all the firstborn were to serve, there would be among them a plague, for the father of this firstborn — perhaps he was not a firstborn, nor his father's father, and they were not habituated to the service. When he comes to serve, he will not be skilled and careful in it, and he will do it improperly and be stricken, as we find with Nadav and Avihu. But the Levites, since they were chosen — they and their sons and their sons' sons for all generations — were habituated and trained in their service to perform it properly. Therefore Scripture says, 'the Levite shall have no portion and inheritance,' etc., so that they not engage in any trade but only in the sacred service, lest his hands learn secular craft, and as a result his arms and fingers become thick and hard, unable to bend and finger the nevel and the kinor, and so ruin the song." "<b>To perform the service of the children of Israel.</b> The service that was incumbent upon the children of Israel to perform." "<b>And there shall be no plague among the children of Israel.</b> Upon any one of the children of Israel — as in 'if he does not do any of these three for her' (Deut. 22:19) and 'in the cities of Gilead' and 'concerning the city of the ass's colt.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {2 "The three cited phrases appear to be fragmentary illustrations of distributive or partitive constructions (באחד = upon any one), but the second and third are textually difficult; rendered hyper-literally as they stand."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:17:05.555742290Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}